Q: Replace first level array keys using values from a specific column A: <h4>PHP 5.5+:</h4>
<p>The new function <a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.array-column.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>array_column</code></a> is very versatile and one of the things it can do is exactly this type of reindexing:</p>
<pre><code>// second parameter is null means we 're just going to reindex the input
$usersById = array_column($users, null, 'Id');
</code></pre>
<h4>Original answer (for PHP versions older than 5.5):</h4>
<p>You need to fetch the ids from the sub-arrays with <a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.array-map.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>array_map</code></a>, then create a new array with <a href="http://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-combine.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>array_combine</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>$ids = array_map(function($user) { return $user['Id']; }, $users);
$users = array_combine($ids, $users);
</code></pre>
<p>The code above requires PHP &gt;= 5.3 for the anonymous function syntax, but you can also do the same (albeit it will look a bit uglier) with <a href="http://www.php.net/manual/en/function.create-function.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>create_function</code></a> which only requires PHP &gt;= 4.0.1:</p>
<pre><code>$ids = array_map(create_function('$user', 'return $user[&quot;Id&quot;];'), $users);
$users = array_combine($ids, $users);
</code></pre>
<p><strong><a href="http://ideone.com/ZIaRq" rel="nofollow noreferrer">See it in action</a></strong>.</p>

Q: How can I set an object&#39;s property value depending on a condition using if statements? A: <p>This is a good example of why &quot;works&quot; and &quot;does not work&quot; are poor descriptions of functionality.  What does the functionality <em>observably do</em>?  In the case of these two examples, both <em>successfully</em> set a property called <code>className</code> on an element.  And you can observe that by examining that element in your debugger, or simply logging the value of that property to the console.</p>
<p><em>But</em> they set <code>className</code> to two <em>very different things</em>.  One sets it to a <em>function</em>:</p>
<pre><code>p.className = (type) =&gt; {
  // code within the function
}
</code></pre>
<p>The other sets it to a <em>string</em>:</p>
<pre><code>p.className = type === 'error' ? 'a value' : 'a different value';
</code></pre>
<p>It should be a string, so the latter one &quot;works&quot;.  Perhaps you meant to <em>invoke</em> your function as an IIFE so the property can be set to the <em>result</em> of that function?  For example:</p>
<pre><code>p.className = ((type) =&gt; {
  // code within the function
})(type);
</code></pre>
<p>This seems unnecessarily confusing to me, and that variable shadowing is a just a bug waiting to happen.  If all you want is to conditionally perform an operation with an <code>if</code> structure then don't over-complicate it.  Just conditionally perform an operation in an <code>if</code> structure:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function log(msg, type) {
  const p = document.createElement("p");
  if (type === 'error') {
    p.className = 'text-red-400 mt-1';
  } else if (type === 'success') {
    p.className = 'text-green-400 mt-1';
  } else {
    p.className = 'text-gray-300 mt-1';
  }
  console.log(p.className);
}

log('foo', 'error');
log('foo', 'success');
log('foo', 'baz');</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>As a personal preference I would generally choose the ternary conditional operator as you've already done.  It makes it more clear (at least to me) that the condition just needs to produce a result and that result is set to a property.  One assignment statement.  That's just personal preference though.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>As an aside... What on earth is <code>console.appendChild</code>?  I changed it to <code>console.log</code> and logged just the specific value in question for any demos above.</p>
</blockquote>
